1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,171
Part one ended with a cleared batch sitting ready to spend against.

2
00:00:04,171 --> 00:00:08,390
This part is where it actually earns a verdict, the same sequence your own

3
00:00:08,390 --> 00:00:13,123
capstone's back half has to run: build the architecture, spend against it, read the

4
00:00:13,123 --> 00:00:15,746
result honestly, and decide what happens next.

5
00:00:15,746 --> 00:00:20,137
Section 7.2 built the actual structure: eight ad sets, one per cleared angle,

6
00:00:20,137 --> 00:00:21,904
twenty-five dollars a day each.

7
00:00:21,904 --> 00:00:25,554
A twenty-four-dollar CAC ceiling, worked from Dana's own margin.

8
00:00:25,554 --> 00:00:28,861
A kill rule at fifty dollars spent with zero add-to-carts.

9
00:00:28,861 --> 00:00:33,423
Every one of those numbers was decided before a single dollar of real spend went

10
00:00:33,423 --> 00:00:36,388
out, exactly the discipline Section 7.2 insisted on.

11
00:00:36,388 --> 00:00:38,327
Notice the order this happened in.

12
00:00:38,327 --> 00:00:42,604
The numbers came first, written down, before Sam looked at a single result.

13
00:00:42,604 --> 00:00:47,222
That's the opposite of picking a CAC ceiling that happens to make an early result

14
00:00:47,222 --> 00:00:47,793
look good.

15
00:00:47,793 --> 00:00:52,526
By the time real data existed, there was no room left to quietly move the goalposts

16
00:00:52,526 --> 00:00:55,263
to fit whatever the account was doing that week.

17
00:00:55,263 --> 00:00:59,938
A number decided in advance is a genuine commitment, checkable by anyone who looks

18
00:00:59,938 --> 00:01:00,623
at it later.

19
00:01:00,623 --> 00:01:05,299
A number decided after results are already visible is just a story dressed up as a

20
00:01:05,299 --> 00:01:08,093
rule, bent to whatever answer already felt right.

21
00:01:08,643 --> 00:01:12,009
Two hundred dollars a day, total, across all eight ad sets.

22
00:01:12,009 --> 00:01:16,744
That's the real number this whole test cost to run, against a margin structure that

23
00:01:16,744 --> 00:01:17,656
could absorb it.

24
00:01:17,656 --> 00:01:22,277
Notice this table has no line for "which angle will win." That question genuinely

25
00:01:22,277 --> 00:01:26,613
couldn't be answered yet, and Section 7's entire structure exists to make it

26
00:01:26,613 --> 00:01:29,922
answerable honestly once real spend has actually happened.

27
00:01:29,922 --> 00:01:34,657
Every one of these four numbers traces back to an earlier lecture, not a guess made

28
00:01:34,657 --> 00:01:35,456
in the moment.

29
00:01:35,456 --> 00:01:39,107
The budget and CAC ceiling came from Section 1.5's margin maths.

30
00:01:39,107 --> 00:01:43,899
The kill rule came from Section 7.2's reasoning about what "no signal at all" should

31
00:01:43,899 --> 00:01:44,812
cost to confirm.

32
00:01:44,812 --> 00:01:48,406
Nothing here was invented specifically for this worked example.

33
00:01:48,406 --> 00:01:52,913
Compare this against your own capstone's numbers, if you've reached this stage.

34
00:01:52,913 --> 00:01:56,849
Yours won't match Harlan's, and that's correct, not a problem to fix.

35
00:01:56,849 --> 00:01:59,587
A different margin produces a different ceiling.

36
00:01:59,587 --> 00:02:04,379
What should match is the discipline: worked from your own real figures, written down

37
00:02:04,379 --> 00:02:07,745
before any result existed to tempt you into adjusting them.

38
00:02:08,295 --> 00:02:11,993
One winner, against seven ad sets that mostly landed on noise.

39
00:02:11,993 --> 00:02:16,883
That ratio isn't a disappointing result, it's what Section 8.2 already told you an

40
00:02:16,883 --> 00:02:18,314
honest batch looks like.

41
00:02:18,314 --> 00:02:23,144
Two point six percent click-through, nearly three times the old gut-era baseline,

42
00:02:23,144 --> 00:02:27,676
and a CAC comfortably under the ceiling rather than barely scraping past it.

43
00:02:27,676 --> 00:02:30,360
Notice what earned this creative its verdict.

44
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:35,309
Not one flattering number in isolation, all three questions from Section 8.2's tree

45
00:02:35,309 --> 00:02:40,140
answered honestly in sequence: real margin past the kill rule, a genuine gap past

46
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:44,731
the CAC ceiling, and survival through the swap test that catches a result too

47
00:02:44,731 --> 00:02:45,984
fragile to trust yet.

48
00:02:45,984 --> 00:02:50,754
The other seven ad sets don't get a dramatic callout here, and that's deliberate

49
00:02:50,754 --> 00:02:50,993
too.

50
00:02:50,993 --> 00:02:55,943
Six or seven quiet noise verdicts sitting alongside one winner is exactly the ratio

51
00:02:55,943 --> 00:03:00,654
Section 8.2 told you to expect, not a sign the other angles were poorly chosen.

52
00:03:00,654 --> 00:03:05,484
Most of a real batch losing cleanly to noise is the honest, ordinary outcome, not

53
00:03:05,484 --> 00:03:06,319
the exception.

54
00:03:06,319 --> 00:03:11,030
Read without Section 8's tree behind it, two point six percent alone might have

55
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:15,860
looked convincing enough to call a winner on day one, before the kill rule or the

56
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:17,470
swap test ever got applied.

57
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:22,241
The tree exists precisely to slow that instinct down until a result has actually

58
00:03:22,241 --> 00:03:22,837
earned it.

59
00:03:23,387 --> 00:03:28,154
This is Section 8.3 and 8.4's entire argument, compressed into one account's real

60
00:03:28,154 --> 00:03:28,684
timeline.

61
00:03:28,684 --> 00:03:33,392
The same winning creative went tired, and the fix wasn't the kill rule, it was a

62
00:03:33,392 --> 00:03:37,629
refresh: same angle, new cut, tested again rather than trusted on faith.

63
00:03:37,629 --> 00:03:41,337
That refresh recovering is what actually licensed the scale-up.

64
00:03:41,337 --> 00:03:46,221
Four hundred dollars a day is a real core-campaign budget, and it's only defensible

65
00:03:46,221 --> 00:03:51,047
because a twenty-one-dollar CAC sits underneath it, tested and proven, not assumed

66
00:03:51,047 --> 00:03:54,637
from a feeling about which creative deserved to keep running.

67
00:03:54,637 --> 00:03:58,638
Notice this refresh followed Section 8.4's Pattern A, not Pattern B.

68
00:03:58,638 --> 00:04:03,287
It recovered close to the original cut's performance rather than fading fast or

69
00:04:03,287 --> 00:04:04,818
failing to recover at all.

70
00:04:04,818 --> 00:04:08,407
That's why the angle kept scaling instead of getting retired.

71
00:04:08,407 --> 00:04:13,057
A different refresh, one that fatigued again quickly, would have sent this same

72
00:04:13,057 --> 00:04:17,529
account back to Section 2.4's angle map for something genuinely new instead.

73
00:04:17,529 --> 00:04:22,472
Hold the whole arc together for a second: twenty-five dollars a day, a handful of ad

74
00:04:22,472 --> 00:04:23,591
sets, mostly noise.

75
00:04:23,591 --> 00:04:25,474
One winner, recognised honestly.

76
00:04:25,474 --> 00:04:28,475
Fatigue, caught by frequency rather than a feeling.

77
00:04:28,475 --> 00:04:31,065
A refresh, tested again rather than trusted.

78
00:04:31,065 --> 00:04:33,889
Then, and only then, four hundred dollars a day.

79
00:04:33,889 --> 00:04:35,714
Every step earned the next one.

80
00:04:35,714 --> 00:04:38,185
None of it was skipped to get here faster.

81
00:04:38,735 --> 00:04:41,624
One honest note before you finish your own capstone.

82
00:04:41,624 --> 00:04:45,790
The gap between "eight ad sets launched" and "one confirmed winner" in this

83
00:04:45,790 --> 00:04:48,122
walkthrough is a few minutes of narration.

84
00:04:48,122 --> 00:04:52,233
On the real account it was days of accumulating spend before Section 7.4's

85
00:04:52,233 --> 00:04:56,565
small-sample honesty allowed any verdict at all, and weeks more before fatigue

86
00:04:56,565 --> 00:04:59,454
showed up and the refresh had to prove itself again.

87
00:04:59,454 --> 00:05:04,120
If your own capstone's test is still sitting in noise after a few days, that's not a

88
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:05,397
sign something's wrong.

89
00:05:05,397 --> 00:05:10,063
It's Section 7.4's discipline doing its job, refusing to hand you a verdict the data

90
00:05:10,063 --> 00:05:11,563
hasn't actually earned yet.

91
00:05:11,563 --> 00:05:14,340
Would you rather have a fast answer or a true one?

92
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:18,784
Every shortcut this course has warned against, guessing a winner from a feeling,

93
00:05:18,784 --> 00:05:23,394
reading a result before enough spend accumulated, trades a true answer for a faster

94
00:05:23,394 --> 00:05:23,616
one.

95
00:05:23,616 --> 00:05:28,226
Harlan's account only reached a genuine scaled winner by consistently choosing true

96
00:05:28,226 --> 00:05:30,837
over fast, at every single one of these stages.

97
00:05:30,837 --> 00:05:33,836
Notice, too, that patience here didn't mean passivity.

98
00:05:33,836 --> 00:05:38,058
Waiting for an honest read isn't the same as doing nothing while it arrives.

99
00:05:38,058 --> 00:05:42,557
Section 9's weekly sprint kept running the whole time, scoring, checking fatigue,

100
00:05:42,557 --> 00:05:47,167
keeping the swipe file stocked, so the account was never simply idle while the data

101
00:05:47,167 --> 00:05:48,611
slowly became trustworthy.

102
00:05:49,161 --> 00:05:54,002
Next lecture is the rubric, a direct checklist against both worked passes so you can

103
00:05:54,002 --> 00:05:58,613
score your own capstone honestly rather than just guessing whether it went well.

104
00:05:58,613 --> 00:06:03,223
Run your own test at whatever pace your real spend allows, even if that's slower

105
00:06:03,223 --> 00:06:05,183
than either of these two lectures.

106
00:06:05,183 --> 00:06:09,793
A verdict rushed past Section 7.4's honesty check isn't a faster version of this

107
00:06:09,793 --> 00:06:10,254
process.

108
00:06:10,254 --> 00:06:12,213
It just isn't this process at all.

109
00:06:12,213 --> 00:06:16,882
Take a moment, before the rubric, to notice what these two worked passes actually

110
00:06:16,882 --> 00:06:18,149
demonstrated together.

111
00:06:18,149 --> 00:06:21,031
Not a single clever trick, not one lucky creative.

112
00:06:21,031 --> 00:06:25,757
A sequence of ordinary, checkable decisions, each one traceable back to a specific

113
00:06:25,757 --> 00:06:28,869
lecture, that happened to compound into a real result.

114
00:06:28,869 --> 00:06:33,652
That compounding, not any individual step, is the entire thing this course has been

115
00:06:33,652 --> 00:06:34,574
trying to teach.
